.TH MORSEDEC-LIVE 1 "2026-10-16" "libmorsedec 0.2.0" "General Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
morsedec-live \- decode streaming Morse audio from standard input
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B morsedec-live
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B morsedec-live
decodes headerless, interleaved audio PCM read from standard input. It does not
open audio devices; use a recorder such as
.B arecord
to supply the stream. Input is processed until end of file, a termination
signal, or the optional input duration limit.
.PP
By default, messages from the selected channel are written to standard output
as text snapshots. Use
.B -a
to print all tracked channels or
.B -j
for JSON Lines snapshots, including revisions. Diagnostics are written to
standard error. An optional CSV trace records per-hop channel metrics, and
.B -T
replaces the text snapshots with a full-screen terminal view.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI -p " N"
Maximum processing participants including the caller. Zero (default) selects
automatic channel parallelism; one creates no workers. Automatic mode uses
online
logical CPUs capped by channel capacity, keeping fewer than four runnable
channels serial. Explicit values permit parallel processing from two channels.
FFT and band management remain serial. Automatic worker startup failure falls
back to serial; explicit requests report errors. Small workloads can run faster
with one participant. N must be a nonnegative decimal integer.
.TP
.BI -r " HZ"
Set the input sample rate. Required. The value is samples per second.
.TP
.BI -F " s16|f32"
Set the sample format. The default is
.B s16
(signed 16-bit little-endian PCM). The alternative
.B f32
is little-endian IEEE binary32.
.TP
.BI -n " N"
Set the number of interleaved audio channels. The default is one.
.TP
.BI -c " N"
Select audio channel
.I N
(1-based). By default, channels are averaged.
.TP
.BI -f " HZ"
Pin decoding to a positive audio frequency. Without this option, signals are
discovered automatically.
.TP
.BI -l " HZ"
Set the lower retained band edge. The default is 200 Hz.
.TP
.BI -u " HZ"
Set the exclusive upper retained band edge. The default is 1200 Hz.
.TP
.BI -w " WPM"
Set the initial Morse speed estimate, from 5 through 80 WPM. The default uses
model priors.
.TP
.BI -s " N"
Stop after consuming
.I N
seconds worth of input samples. This limits input duration, not wall-clock time.
.TP
.BI -a
Print messages from all tracked channels instead of only the selected channel.
.TP
.B -j
Write JSON Lines message snapshots, including revisions, instead of human
readable text.
.TP
.B -T
Show the decode in a full-screen terminal view instead of writing text
snapshots to standard output. The left pane lists every tracked channel with
its frequency, estimated SNR, speed and state; the right pane shows one
scrollable row per frequency. See
.B TERMINAL VIEW
below. JSON Lines
.RB ( -j )
and the CSV trace
.RB ( -t )
keep working alongside the view. A build configured with
.B -Dtui=disabled
rejects this option.
.TP
.BI -t " FILE"
Write per-hop channel metrics as CSV to
.IR FILE .
.TP
.B -V
Print version, copyright, and warranty information, then exit.
.TP
.B -h
Print a short usage summary, then exit.
.SH INPUT FORMAT
Input is read from standard input as headerless little-endian PCM. It contains
one or more interleaved audio channels according to
.BR -n .
Each frame contains one sample per channel. No resampling is performed. The
sample rate and sample format must match the stream producer. Incomplete sample
frames at end of input are reported as an error.
.SH STREAM HANDLING
The tool retains partial reads between system calls and processes complete
decoder hops as they arrive. End of file, SIGINT, SIGTERM, or the duration limit
flushes pending decoder output before exit. Empty input is valid. A signal is
handled in the main processing loop so pending output and resources can be
drained and released normally.
.PP
On Windows the tool checks pipe data availability at 100 ms intervals, waits
on console input handles and reads regular files directly. It switches
standard input to binary mode. Ctrl-C is delivered by the console
handler; SIGTERM and SIGPIPE do not exist, and a closed downstream pipe becomes
a write error rather than a signal.
.PP
The optional CSV trace includes synthetic drain time at shutdown. PCM input has
no timestamps or discontinuity markers; dropped samples cannot be detected by
the decoder.
.SH TERMINAL VIEW
With
.BR -T ,
a two pane view is drawn on the controlling terminal. The left pane lists every
tracked channel as frequency in Hz, estimated SNR in dB, estimated speed in WPM
and decoder state; the channel the decoder currently follows is marked with a
greater-than sign and kept in view. The right pane keeps one row per
frequency, labelled in Hz.
Updates replace that frequency's text in place, even when other channels decode
between updates. Channels keep their rows as their frequency drifts; new
channels at retained frequencies reuse the existing rows. Long messages show
their newest characters on the same line, with < marking omitted text. Rows
remain in first-seen order until cleared or evicted from the bounded history.
.PP
.B q
or
.B ESC
quits,
.B c
clears the message pane,
.B Up
and
.B Down
scroll one line,
.B PgUp
and
.B PgDn
scroll one screen,
.B Home
jumps to the oldest retained line and
.B End
returns to the live end. The footer shows the retained line count and whether
the view is scrolled back.
.PP
The view takes over the terminal, not standard input, which keeps carrying the
sample stream. On Windows it attaches to the console, so it needs a real
console rather than a pipe. Text snapshots are suppressed while the view is
active, but
.B -j
and
.B -t
still write to standard output and to their file. Terminal support comes from
ncurses and is enabled by default; configure with
.B -Dtui=disabled
to build the tools without it.
.SH EXAMPLES
Decode a mono 48 kHz recording:
.PP
.nf
morsedec-live -r 48000 < recording.s16
.fi
.PP
Capture stereo audio and select the second channel:
.PP
.nf
arecord -t raw -f S16_LE -r 44100 -c 2 | morsedec-live -r 44100 -n 2 -c 2 -j
.fi
.PP
Watch a live capture in the terminal view:
.PP
.nf
arecord -t raw -f S16_LE -r 48000 -c 1 | morsedec-live -r 48000 -T
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 after successful decoding and output, or a nonzero status if input,
configuration, decoding, or output fails.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR morsedec-audio (1),
.BR morsedec-iqlive (1),
.BR libmorsedec (3)
